## 4. What I'd do Monday morning

1. **Timing pass (30 min, $0).** Render the WALKTHROUGH.md script as
   a TTS scratch track; mark the beat timings on the shot list; fix
   any line that doesn't survive being spoken.
2. **Connect + capture setup (30 min).** Install OBS on the recording
   machine; build two scenes (browser, terminal); browser zoom set
   for text legibility; test-record the coordinate jump and watch it
   back at 50% size — if the text isn't readable small, it isn't
   readable on YouTube.
3. **Capture the deterministic blocks first** (per WALKTHROUGH.md §5):
   terminal demo, plain-editor raw-file scroll — they never change
   between takes.
4. **Browser block at edit.phext.io**, working the confirm-during-
   recording list as I go (demo phext offered to a signed-out
   visitor, exact UI labels, download/export reality for the 5:15
   beat — fallback already scripted).
5. **Will records narration** against the scratch timing — two takes,
   keep both.
6. **Edit in Descript:** assemble to the narration, zooms/callouts on
   the UI beats, stack-diagram cutaway, chapter markers from §2,
   script-derived captions; drop the foreshadow still + push-in at
   7:45 (art pulled from the Scrolltown pipeline, §2 above).
7. **Package:** thumbnail in Canva from a real frame; optional
   15-minute vidIQ scoring pass on title + thumbnail; export master
   (1080p) and the X cutdown (burned-in captions); ffmpeg-verify both
   files (ffprobe: streams, duration, resolution).
8. **Approval gate:** Will sees the exact video, thumbnail, title,
   description, and X copy. Only then: YouTube Studio upload, then
   the X thread. Nothing in steps 1–7 posts anything.

